{"content_hash": "3baae24bb7351cb2", "version_id": 206, "article_id": 21, "level_code": "ja_n5", "lang": "ja", "level": 1, "level_label": "N5 初級", "title": "スペインで87さいの女の人がなくなりました", "title_zh": "西班牙87岁老太太去世了", "topic": "world", "source": "bbc_world", "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6e3x21ev9wwo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/standard/240/cpsprodpb/d9ee/live/06010330-c283-11f1-98ea-35e6bf307fc9.jpg", "credit": ""}, "published_date": "2026-10-08", "word_count": 288, "reading_minutes": 0.9, "lead": "スペインのマドリードで、87さいの女の人がなくなりました。この人は家を出なければならなくて、国中大騒ぎになりました。", "lead_zh": "西班牙马德里一位87岁的老太太去世了。她被迫搬离住所，引发了全国范围的抗议。", "preview": "女の人の名前はマリカルメン・アバスカルさんです。87さいでした。9月23日に、家を出されました。70年以上住んでいました。家賃が上がって、払えなくなりました。", "preview_zh": "这位女士名叫玛丽卡门·阿巴斯卡，87岁。9月23日，她被赶出了家门。她在那里住了70多年。房租上涨，她付不起了。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 196, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@ab49ecc/content/audio/21/ja_n5.mp3", "duration": 68.85, "size_bytes": 416880, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "ja-JP-NanamiNeural", "word_count": 211, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@ab49ecc/content/data/versions/206.json", "paragraphs": ["女の人の名前はマリカルメン・アバスカルさんです。87さいでした。9月23日に、家を出されました。70年以上住んでいました。家賃が上がって、払えなくなりました。", "家の会社は、安い家賃でまた住んでいいと言いました。しかし、アバスカルさんは入院していて、契約できませんでした。10月7日に亡くなりました。", "マドリードの広場に、たくさんの人が集まりました。ろうそくをともしました。そして、1分間黙りました。「戦いは続く」と書いた紙を持った人もいました。", "人々は「もう立ち退きは許さない」と叫びました。警察も来ました。テントの生活は12日目です。ある人は「必要なら続ける」と言いました。", "スペインの首相は、11月29日に選挙をすると言いました。家の問題で、政治も動いています。"], "paragraphs_zh": ["这位女士名叫玛丽卡门·阿巴斯卡，87岁。9月23日，她被赶出了家门。她在那里住了70多年。房租上涨，她付不起了。", "房东公司说可以让她以较低的租金继续住。但阿巴斯卡住院了，没法签合同。10月7日，她去世了。", "很多人聚集在马德里的广场上。他们点了蜡烛，并默哀了一分钟。还有人举着写有“斗争将继续”的牌子。", "人们高喊“再也不能容忍任何一次驱逐”。警察也来了。帐篷抗议已经进入第12天。有人说“只要有需要就会继续”。", "西班牙首相表示将在11月29日举行大选。住房问题也引发了政治变动。"], "body": "女の人の名前はマリカルメン・アバスカルさんです。87さいでした。9月23日に、家を出されました。70年以上住んでいました。家賃が上がって、払えなくなりました。\n\n家の会社は、安い家賃でまた住んでいいと言いました。しかし、アバスカルさんは入院していて、契約できませんでした。10月7日に亡くなりました。\n\nマドリードの広場に、たくさんの人が集まりました。ろうそくをともしました。そして、1分間黙りました。「戦いは続く」と書いた紙を持った人もいました。\n\n人々は「もう立ち退きは許さない」と叫びました。警察も来ました。テントの生活は12日目です。ある人は「必要なら続ける」と言いました。\n\nスペインの首相は、11月29日に選挙をすると言いました。家の問題で、政治も動いています。", "body_zh": "这位女士名叫玛丽卡门·阿巴斯卡，87岁。9月23日，她被赶出了家门。她在那里住了70多年。房租上涨，她付不起了。\n\n房东公司说可以让她以较低的租金继续住。但阿巴斯卡住院了，没法签合同。10月7日，她去世了。\n\n很多人聚集在马德里的广场上。他们点了蜡烛，并默哀了一分钟。还有人举着写有“斗争将继续”的牌子。\n\n人们高喊“再也不能容忍任何一次驱逐”。警察也来了。帐篷抗议已经进入第12天。有人说“只要有需要就会继续”。\n\n西班牙首相表示将在11月29日举行大选。住房问题也引发了政治变动。", "text": "女の人の名前はマリカルメン・アバスカルさんです。87さいでした。9月23日に、家を出されました。70年以上住んでいました。家賃が上がって、払えなくなりました。 家の会社は、安い家賃でまた住んでいいと言いました。しかし、アバスカルさんは入院していて、契約できませんでした。10月7日に亡くなりました。 マドリードの広場に、たくさんの人が集まりました。ろうそくをともしました。そして、1分間黙りました。「戦いは続く」と書いた紙を持った人もいました。 人々は「もう立ち退きは許さない」と叫びました。警察も来ました。テントの生活は12日目です。ある人は「必要なら続ける」と言いました。 スペインの首相は、11月29日に選挙をすると言いました。家の問題で、政治も動いています。", "vocab": [{"word": "女の人", "pos": "noun", "zh": "女人", "note": "礼貌的说法"}, {"word": "なくなる", "pos": "verb", "zh": "去世", "note": "委婉说法"}, {"word": "家", "pos": "noun", "zh": "家、房子", "note": ""}, {"word": "家賃", "pos": "noun", "zh": "房租", "note": ""}, {"word": "上がる", "pos": "verb", "zh": "上涨", "note": "自动词"}, {"word": "入院する", "pos": "verb", "zh": "住院", "note": ""}, {"word": "ろうそく", "pos": "noun", "zh": "蜡烛", "note": ""}, {"word": "選挙", "pos": "noun", "zh": "选举", "note": ""}], "title_original": "Spanish pensioner whose eviction sparked nationwide protests dies, union says", "summary_original": "Maricarmen Abascal, 87, was forcibly removed on a stretcher from her apartment of more than 70 years in September.", "body_original": "Spanish pensioner whose eviction sparked nationwide protests dies, union says\nBy\nAlys Davies\nand\nAlba Morgade in Madrid\nPublished\n7 October 2026\nUpdated 59 minutes ago\nAn elderly woman whose eviction sparked nationwide protests over Spain's housing crisis and a political row that triggered early elections has died, according to Madrid's tenants union.\nMaricarmen Abascal, 87, was forcibly removed on a stretcher from her apartment of more than 70 years on 23 September after she was unable to afford rent increases.\nThe company that owned the flat later offered to allow her to return at a lower rate - but she was unable to sign an agreement as she had been admitted to hospital.\n\"We regret to announce that Maricarmen passed away today,\" the Madrid Tenants' Union said in a statement, adding her situation marked \"a turning point in the history of this country\".\nUnion spokesperson Alicia del Río said: \"Speculation costs lives, and today rentierism has finished killing our colleague Maricarmen\" but \"her memory lives on with us\".\nA cause of death was not announced.\nAbascal's death was received in Puerta del Sol with a big collective gasp, then followed by a minute of silence.\nIt is in this square in the centre of Madrid where thousands have been camping since Abascal's eviction last month to demand more affordable housing.\nOn Wednesday, it became the place to pay tribute to her.\nPeople lit dozens of candles around the square in a small improvised vigil that glowed with a warm light on a dry and warm autumn night.\nOther candles formed an altar to Abascal in front of the headquarters of the Community of Madrid government.\nMessages on cardboard banners next to the candles read \"it's not a death, it's a killing\", \"the fight will continue\" and \"Mari Carmen, the grandmother of the whole country\", among others.\nA few hundred people then took to the streets that connect Puerta del Sol with the Spanish Congress, just 500m away, in an improvised protest.\nDuring the march people chanted: \"We will not tolerate a single eviction more\" and \"the fight will continue, Mari Carmen is still alive\". There were other chants against fascism and against the police.\nThe police had set up barriers to block the march. There was not a big police presence, and there was the tension that protests sometimes have.\nMany protesters seemed to feel the police have been complicit in the evictions, as under court orders they have to remove tenants who have been served eviction notices.\nAfter a couple of hours, the chants near Congress gave way to another minute of silence. Then, a big collective shout of \"murderers\".\nWednesday night's march has finished, but the camping, already on its 12th night, will continue \"as long as necessary\" one of the protesters told the BBC.\nAbascal became a symbol for Spain's housing crisis following her eviction.\nHer father first rented the property in 1956 and she continued as the tenant after her parents died, during which time there was a cap on the rent.\nHowever, Urbagestión, which bought the property in 2018, increased the rent from €500 per month (£428; $572) to €2,650 (£2,280; $3,020).\nThe company later offered to reduce this to €1,650 - still €300 more than Abascal's monthly pension.\nThree previous attempts to evict her were blocked by the courts and the case has received increasing media attention.\nAnger over the situation grew, leading to protests and putting pressure on the coalition government to approve emergency housing legislation -\nbut it was rejected in Congress last week\n.\nThat led to further protests,\nwith tens of thousands of people taking\nto the streets\nin cities across the country over the weekend.\nReferring to the rejected bills, the Madrid Tenants' Union said: \"While a historic agreement was reached that would have allowed her to return to her home, this solution has come too late. An absolutely avoidable situation after a torturing process.\"\nIt said the events surrounding her eviction represented a turning point in the country's history, but added that the cost to get to this point had been \"too high\".\n\"Above all, we will fulfil what she always asked of us: 'fight, keep fighting for what is yours',\" it said.\nIn a video recorded from her hospital bed following her eviction, Maricarmen said: \"I haven't managed to defend my home, but I have fought so that others can learn to fight for what's theirs.\"\n\"All my love goes to Maricarmen's family and loved ones,\" Spanish Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez said. \"A hug as well to everyone who today feels this loss as their own.\"\nOpposition People's Party leader Alberto Núñez Feijóo also sent his condolences to Maricarmen's loved ones, as he said on X: \"No campaign, no parties, no self-interested exploitation. No more stones in the wall.\n\"In the face of a person's death, only respect is in order,\" he added.\nEarlier on Wednesday, unions had called a general strike to take place on 11 November, demanding housing reforms.\nThe public outcry over Abascal's situation has led to political turmoil in Spain.\nOn Monday Sánchez, who leads a leftist coalition that does not hold a majority in parliament, called an early election for 29 November. It followed his failure to get the emergency housing legislation through Congress.\nThe political right had argued that the government's attempt to intervene in the housing market would have led to fewer homes being rented and would have raised prices further.\nSpeaking on Monday, Sánchez said he needed \"a broader progressive majority\" that can \"overcome the resistance of the elites\" to push through reforms.\nOther issues not relating to housing, such as numerous corruption scandals linked to his party, and pressures relating to the cost of living and immigration, have also caused him problems.\nMost opinion polls suggest the conservatives are likely to emerge as the largest party in the next election. However, they would be expected to need the support of the hard-right Vox party to form a government.\nAdditional reporting by Tom McArthur\nRelated topics\nSpain\nMore on this story\nSpanish PM Sánchez calls early election after housing protests\nPublished\n2 days ago\nDramatic eviction of woman aged 87 highlights Spain's housing shortage\nPublished\n23 September", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T01:16:22.071120", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "女", "s"